NO LIBRARY — the builders write every line in raw bpy:
Nothing is prebuilt in the scene workspace: every mesh, material, lamp, world and motion is raw
`bpy` / `bmesh` the builders write.  So describe each thing by what it IS — dimensions, parts,
materials with colours and how they vary (broad wear and fine grain), and the silhouette features a
person would name (a pine's whorled limbs carrying needle cards, a boulder's irregular facets) —
never by a call or a module.  A thing repeated across zones (a tree species, a lamp post, a crate)
is an asset built from scratch within the triangle budget below.  Zone `contents` lists only actual
asset names; scenery a zone builds itself belongs in its prose, not in invented asset ids.  Water and terrain
share ONE surface sampler (`height_at` from the environment) and every placed thing is seated on
it or on what it stands on — the harness measures the gap.  Geometry that exists only to continue
the world beyond the bounds is named with the `Sky` prefix (the census then keeps it out of the
content bbox).

## What the harness measures and the judge sees (plan so these hold)
- Every authored camera is rendered at t=0 s and t=1.5 s and judged as a picture of the brief's
  place.  `cameras[0]` is measured as the establishing shot: content covers >= 20 % of its frame;
  every other authored camera >= 10 % (the rest being sky/ground).  A lens inside a mesh or
  within 0.5 m of a surface caps the whole run.
- No world edge, horizon seam or box boundary is visible from any authored camera (judge; and
  outdoors the census checks that standing geometry >= 2 m tall reaches past 1.25 x the bounds'
  half-extent, and that the atmosphere's 1 / density is >= the plan span / 1.5 — a denser fog
  dissolves the world inside the content and reads as an edge).  How the world continues is the environment builder's choice for this
  place.
- The ground is a believable landform for the place (judge): flat, sloped, terraced, a shore, a
  cliff — whatever the brief implies; every placed thing is seated on it or on what it stands on
  (placement gate: a gap > 0.05 m outdoors / 0.02 m indoors is floating, > 0.10 m into the
  support is sunken, overlapping neighbours interpenetrate — each caps the score).
- The lighting reads as the stated time of day and weather, and the frame is not monochrome
  unless the brief asks for it (judge; exposure is measured against a luminance band).
- Something moves visibly between t=0 s and t=1.5 s (motion probe), and motion the brief names
  is visible from the establishing camera.
- Repeated elements do not read as identical copies (judge), and >= 8 same-size copies spaced
  evenly on a ring round the world are measured as a stamped backdrop.  A deliberately regular
  row the brief implies (an orchard, a colonnade, bollards along a quay) is fine.
- The place carries what its activity implies; a bare diorama on one plane is a judged defect,
  and a zone under half its layout's count gets an INFO note (the counts the brief states are acceptance items).
- The brief's must-haves are acceptance items; an unmet `must` caps the run at 0.60.

WHAT TO PRODUCE (JSON, fields: {{ schema_fields }}):
1. `title`, `summary` (two sentences), `setting` (place, era, weather, time of day), `mood`.
2. `bounds` — centre + extents in meters of the DETAILED area: where the builders spend their
   detail and what the harness measures against (zone layouts, placed assets — one sitting
   entirely outside is noted — and camera clearance).  It is not a cage: the world continues past
   it as the place does — a road or river running out, a wall, a shoreline, far buildings,
   terrain that keeps going — and the environment builder builds that continuation.  Size and
   proportion it to the brief's subject: a street is long and narrow, a courtyard small, a
   valley wide; a bigger box is not more detail.
3. `environment` — ONE paragraph of concrete numbers for what the brief's place needs:
   the sun / moon azimuth (compass: 0 = +X, 90 = +Y) + elevation in degrees and the time of day
   (dawn / morning / noon / golden / overcast / dusk / night — the builders' lighting table); the
   Sun lamp's colour + energy in W/m² and the sky world strength such that the judged frames land
   in the measured luminance band (pairs that did: day 3-5 W/m² at world 0.4-0.5, dusk 1-1.5 at
   0.2, a moon 0.1-0.3 at 0.1), coloured for the weather; atmosphere: fog density + colour as a
   volume over the ground when the weather needs it (the census reads its density: 1 / density
   >= the plan span / 1.5); the LANDFORM — what the land does across the bounds and its height at each
   authored camera position (camera heights are absolute) — and the ground surface(s) with
   colours (RGB 0-1) and where walkways, roads or tracks run, as (x, z) routes with widths, when
   the brief has them (the zone layouts read them as `path_points`); how the world continues
   beyond the bounds in each direction and what the horizon is from the authored cameras (for an
   interior: what is seen through the openings); water height + colour, if any.  Ponds, terraces
   and beds meet the terrain with no floating edge (placement gate).
4. `zones` — the brief's places, one builder each, with PascalCase names, a `bbox` (where that
   builder may place: layouts are validated against it with 1 m of slack) and `contents` = asset
   names placed there.  Lay them out as the place is laid out — along a street, round a corner,
   radially about a well, scattered over a slope, one inside another — in any number and size;
   they need not cover the bounds, open ground between them is allowed, and two bboxes overlap by
   at most 1 m so their placements cannot collide.  Each zone `description` states, as numbers:
   - what the place is and what happens there, and which authored cameras see it;
   - the layout: what stands where, spacing in meters, what faces what, what stands ON what;
   - the counts the brief fixes, and how much loose dressing this zone carries beyond its
     contents, as the place needs (the zone layout counts it in three fields — mid props, small
     props, ground cover — and the census notes a zone under half its count): a market is crowded,
     a quarry floor nearly bare, zero is a valid answer;
   - how repeated natural elements vary (silhouette, scale, hue) or why a regular row is right.
5. `assets` — the reusable things the brief needs; each is one builder session inside the run's
   time and cost ceiling.  PascalCase `name`; `kind` = `bpy` (a procedural module placed as
   collection instances) or `blender_glb` (built as an object and shipped as a GLB: slower and
   costlier, so for the hero that needs sculpted or bevelled detail; a hero may also carry ONE
   2 s keyframed motion — say so in its description); `description` with construction numbers
   and colours; `approx_size_m` = width x height x depth in meters (the built asset is measured
   against it: a stone lantern 0.5 x 1.6 x 0.5, a bench 1.6 x 0.9 x 0.6, a pine 3 x 7 x 3);
   `instances_hint` = how many appear in total.  Triangle budget: <= 15 000 triangles per module,
   <= 40 000 for a hero (measured by the build census).  Variation belongs inside an asset (say
   "5 variants via variant: three trunk shapes, two crown shapes") rather than in near-duplicate
   modules.  Ground cover, terrain, water, sky and whatever continues the world beyond the bounds
   are built by the environment / zone builders, not as assets.
6. `effects` — 0 or more: `glsl_material` (a shader-node effect: water ripple, lantern glow, a
   translucent leaf), `particles` (petals, sparks, snow — a small keyframed instance cloud),
   `animated_geometry`, or `postprocess` (a volume: mist, light shafts); say which zone/asset owns
   each.
7. `animation` — what moves, each line with an amplitude visible in a 1024x576 frame between t=0
   and t=1.5 s (motion probe).  Amplitudes that have measured as visible: foliage sway
   +/-0.10-0.20 rad; water ripples scrolling >= 0.15 m/s or a bob >= 0.04 m; particles travelling
   >= 0.6 m per 1.5 s; vehicles/boats/sails >= 1.5 m or a visible turn per 1.5 s; lantern flicker
   x0.75-x1.3.  Motion the brief names is visible from the establishing camera.
8. `cameras` — 1 to 6 authored shots; the harness uses your numbers LITERALLY, so compute them
   from where the subject is and how big it is.  `cameras[0]` is measured as the establishing
   shot: it shows the brief's subject clearly, with content >= 20 % of the frame.  Every camera
   stands above the terrain at its own (x, z), keeps its lens >= 0.5 m from any surface and
   looks at content (>= 10 %).  `purpose` says what the shot is of and what the judge should see
   in it.  `fov` (vertical) is yours (schema 5-150).  How many shots, from where and at what height
   is the brief's: a long open vista, a close interior, a walk along a street or a top-down court are
   each right when the place is.
9. `acceptance` — one item per must-have in the brief, plus the measured checks: nothing floating
   or sunken (`how: probe`), the named motion visible between t=0 and t=1.5 s (`probe`), no world
   edge from any authored camera (`visual`), lighting reads as the stated time of day (`visual`).
   **`priority` matters more than anything else in this list.**  ANY unmet `must` item caps the
   whole run's score at 0.60, however good the pictures are — one over-specified detail throws
   away a passing scene.  So:
   - mark `must` only items that restate a hard requirement of the brief itself, that a person
     can confirm from ONE rendered frame, and that name no incidental detail: "a raked gravel
     area is visible in the zen garden" is a `must`; "raked gravel with concentric rings around
     each rock" is a `should`;
   - `a1` is the premise: the kind of PLACE the brief describes, in the plainest words that
     would be false if the scene were somewhere else — "the view is from inside an enclosed
     workshop", "the courtyard is lit only by its lanterns at night".  Measured 2026-08-25: a
     brief reading "INSIDE a boatbuilder's workshop" was planned as "Coastal boatyard, clear
     dusk", built faithfully as an outdoor yard, passed every gate and scored 0.4653.  When the
     premise is INSIDE a built space, set `interior: true`: the environment module then owns the
     enclosure (floor, walls, ceiling, openings where `environment` puts them; the skeleton
     starts it on the bounds' faces and the builder may reshape it) and the zones dress the
     inside — six interior runs with no owner for walls and roof were each judged "not enclosed,
     a diorama on a flat plane" (0.0-0.3).  When the enclosure itself is glass on a frame (a
     conservatory, greenhouse, palm house, glass atrium) also set `glazed: true`: the walls and
     roof are then glass on a grid that the sun and the outside come through;
   - everything else is `priority: should` — craft goals, counts, specific patterns, exact
     heights and anything needing evidence the judge may not have;
   - a `must` whose wording carries a number, a named style, or "and" joining two independent
     claims is rarely confirmable from one frame: split it or demote it.

RULES
- Every name in `zones[].contents` is an asset name; asset names unique; zone names unique.
- Realistic scale: people 1.7 m, doors 2.1 m, steps 0.17 m, benches 0.45 m seat height.
- No prose outside the JSON.

